1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,075
This is a real table tennis rally, from a game our model never saw in training.

2
00:00:04,095 --> 00:00:07,716
It calls this shot a miss 408 milliseconds before the ball gets there.

3
00:00:07,736 --> 00:00:11,923
Courtside asks what that head start is worth on a prediction market, and what we can prove.

4
00:00:12,423 --> 00:00:14,688
When a point ends, the news spreads in tiers.

5
00:00:14,708 --> 00:00:18,511
A courtside camera with our vision model would know in about 95 milliseconds.

6
00:00:18,531 --> 00:00:23,977
ESPN's live score trails the order book by about 28 seconds, the public scoreboard by 45.

7
00:00:23,997 --> 00:00:29,580
Slower tiers trade against stale prices. The gap persists because speed costs money, and capacity is small.

8
00:00:30,080 --> 00:00:33,423
A small group of wallets trades in the first three seconds after a point.

9
00:00:33,443 --> 00:00:38,725
Thirty seconds later, net of fees, they are up in 9 of 9 months. Everyone else is down in 9.

10
00:00:38,745 --> 00:00:43,121
Copying them three seconds later loses in 9 of 9. The edge goes to whoever is first.

11
00:00:43,621 --> 00:00:45,240
So we built our own fast tier.

12
00:00:45,260 --> 00:00:48,909
On held-out games, it tracks the ball and calls misses before contact.

13
00:00:48,929 --> 00:00:55,851
Fifty milliseconds out, it made 11 calls, all correct. The 95% lower bound on precision is 74 percent.

14
00:00:55,871 --> 00:00:59,562
It catches 27 percent of misses that early, and leaves the rest alone.

15
00:01:00,062 --> 00:01:03,877
We have no licensed tennis footage, so this replay is simulated physics.

16
00:01:03,897 --> 00:01:10,037
A 340 frame per second tracker calls it out 200 milliseconds before the bounce, and reads the spin from the flight.

17
00:01:10,057 --> 00:01:14,547
On the right is a real Polymarket tape: fast-tier wallets traded within seconds of the point.

18
00:01:15,047 --> 00:01:18,841
Version two trades only at the fast tier's own fill prices, after fees.

19
00:01:18,861 --> 00:01:26,189
In sample, February to August: 1.38 cents a share, Sharpe 14.5, worst drawdown 2.0 percent.

20
00:01:26,209 --> 00:01:31,779
Out of sample, a window we had already seen: 0.60 cents, Sharpe 6.7.

21
00:01:31,799 --> 00:01:36,222
Double the fees, and out of sample turns negative: −0.34 cents.

22
00:01:36,722 --> 00:01:40,393
We tried thousands of configurations, so we deflate the Sharpe ratio.

23
00:01:40,413 --> 00:01:45,950
In sample it stays above 0.99, even counting all 3,386 trials.

24
00:01:45,970 --> 00:01:50,692
The 40-day out-of-sample window does not survive that. It falls to 0.07.

25
00:01:50,712 --> 00:01:54,879
Probability of backtest overfitting on the 24-variant grid: 15 percent.

26
00:01:55,379 --> 00:02:00,966
Unseen markets passed in sample, then failed out of sample. The market-making variant failed its blind test.

27
00:02:00,986 --> 00:02:03,399
Table tennis markets had no fast tier at all.

28
00:02:03,419 --> 00:02:08,566
The blind forward test is still pending. Every look at out-of-sample data is logged: 51 entries.

29
00:02:09,066 --> 00:02:11,857
What does vision buy? This part is a counterfactual.

30
00:02:11,877 --> 00:02:16,424
It assumes a licensed feed and a courtside camera we did not buy, with parameters we measured.

31
00:02:16,444 --> 00:02:24,270
In sample, 1.10 cents a share, Sharpe 11.4. Out of sample, 0.58 cents, Sharpe 7.3.

32
00:02:24,290 --> 00:02:28,719
The sign hinges on how fast the market reprices after the bounce, which we have not measured.

33
00:02:28,739 --> 00:02:33,084
Under the stamp reading, if that takes less than 1.1 seconds, the edge is gone.

34
00:02:33,584 --> 00:02:37,221
The engine runs end to end on live Polymarket order books, paper only.

35
00:02:37,241 --> 00:02:42,684
In a 60 second check it read 7,785 book messages across 27 markets,

36
00:02:42,704 --> 00:02:47,806
at a median feed latency of 65 milliseconds, with zero mismatches and zero orders sent.

37
00:02:47,826 --> 00:02:49,920
Tonight's live paper session is running now.

38
00:02:50,420 --> 00:02:56,768
In sample, a gross edge of 282 dollars a day loses 86 to taker fees, leaving 196.

39
00:02:56,788 --> 00:03:04,486
Central fixed costs, mostly a data licence, are 167 a day. That leaves 29 in sample, and a loss out of sample.

40
00:03:04,506 --> 00:03:09,145
Out of sample, the edge stays positive only up to about 34 thousand dollars of capital.

41
00:03:09,165 --> 00:03:13,079
Kill switches stop trading on a drawdown, a stale feed, or a fee change.

42
00:03:13,579 --> 00:03:19,403
Everything here is paper trading. No real money was traded, and no live ATP or WTA data was used.

43
00:03:19,423 --> 00:03:24,378
The code and every result file are in the public repository. One command rebuilds the backtest tables.

